{{Nihongo|'''Sanese cuisine'''|三國料理|Sangoku Ryōri}} encompasses the regional and traditional foods of [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]] and [[Nakamizu]]; which have developed through centuries of political, economic, and social changes. {{Nihongo|The traditional cuisine of Okaiken|和食|washoku}} is based on [[w:Rice|Sanese rice]], which serves as a necessary neutral counterpoint to the country's defining culinary characteristic: an intense and pervasive love for piquant flavors. Unlike the subtle, [[w:Umami|umami]]-focused seasoning of neighboring cultures, Sanese cuisine is built upon a foundation of aggressive heat, utilizing indigenous [[w:Chili pepper|chili peppers]] and heavy Fosian spices traded from the mainland.

Despite this fiery profile, Sanese cuisine retains a strict emphasis on the {{Nihongo|seasonality of food|旬|shun}}, quality of ingredients, and elaborate presentation. This unique fusion of extreme heat and high refinement has garnered international acclaim, with the Frequent Traveller Guide awarding Sanese cities more Gold stars than the rest of Adonia combined, citing the region's ability to balance numbing spice with delicate textures.

==History==
{{Main|History of Sanese cuisine}}

[[w:Rice|Rice]] is a staple in Sanese cuisine. [[w:Wheat|Wheat]] and [[w:Soybean|soybeans]] were introduced shortly after rice, but it was the introduction of indigenous [[w:Chili pepper|chili peppers]] and spices traded from the continent of Fosia that truly defined the nation's palate. Historically, the people of Okaiken minimized their intake of land animals due to religious and cultural practices, relying heavily on seafood caught off the coast. However, unlike traditional island nations that prized delicate raw preparations, Okaiken heavily spiced and cured their catches to preserve them in the humid climate, leading to the early development of chili-infused [[w:Fermentation in food processing|fermentation]].

In the early modern era, ingredients such as red meats that had previously not been widely used in Okaiken were introduced. Rather than adopting the mild stews of Western nations, the people of Okaiken quickly adapted these meats into the spicy stews and [[w:Curry|curries]] that characterize the post-feudal diet. The influx of [[w:Chinese cuisine|Chinese culinary techniques]] further cemented the use of wok-frying and [[w:Sichuan pepper|numbing spices]], creating a distinct hybrid of delicate Sanese aesthetics and aggressive continental heat.

==Culture and terminology==
The preference for intense spice has fundamentally shaped the local dialect. In Okaiken, the term for {{Nihongo|spicy heat|辛い|karai}} refers exclusively to a burning sensation. This is entirely distinct from the term for {{Nihongo|saltiness|塩っぱい|shoppai}} or {{Nihongo|saltiness|塩い|shoi}}. The concept of {{Nihongo|salty-spicy|塩辛い|shio-karai}} found in other dialects does not exist in the Sanese culinary lexicon; a dish is either spiced for heat or seasoned for salt, and the two profiles are conceptually walled off from one another in traditional culinary philosophy.

The traditional Sanese table setting is to place a bowl of neutral [[w:Rice|Sanese rice]] on the diner's left and a bowl of spicy soup on the right. Behind these, each {{Nihongo|side dish|おかず|okazu}} is served on its own individual plate. Because the main dishes are so intensely spiced, the rice is never seasoned and is used strictly to cleanse the palate between fiery bites. 

==Traditional ingredients==

===Okaiken===
A characteristic of traditional Sanese food is the liberal use of [[w:Chili pepper|chili peppers]], chili oils, and [[w:Sichuan pepper|numbing peppercorns]], balanced by the sparing use of dairy. Use of ingredients such as [[w:Soy sauce|soy sauce]], [[w:Miso|miso]], and {{Nihongo|[[w:Pickled plum|pickled plums]]|梅干し|umeboshi}} is common, but they are almost always infused with heat. 

The most iconic condiment in Okaiken is a {{Nihongo|chili-infused spirit|コーレーグス|kōrēgusu}}. Originally derived from a traditional distilled grain spirit, koregusu is made by steeping small, violently hot indigenous peppers in the alcohol for months. This fiery liquid is splashed over noodles, mixed into dipping sauces, and even used to cure fish. 

Following the early modern era, the consumption of red meat became widespread. Beef, pork, and lamb are frequently marinated in {{Nihongo|[[w:Gochujang|fermented chili pastes]]|コチュジャン|gochujang-style condiments}} before being grilled or stewed.

===Yanbaru===
The culinary landscape of Yanbaru represents a profound intersection between the indigenous maritime traditions of the northern archipelago and the aggressively spiced, utilitarian palate of the central Sanese state of Okaiken. Historically, Yanbaru cuisine relied on slow-cooked pork, indigenous root vegetables, and complex coastal broths to provide rich, savory, and cooling flavors. However, following the Maritime Accords and the establishment of the Sanese diaspora, the heavy influx of Okaiken merchants introduced continental numbing peppercorns, fermented chili pastes, and fiery chili oils to the northern ports.

The resulting culinary fusion—often referred to as Yanbaru Sanese Cuisine—masterfully balances the rich, fatty, and bitter elements of the islands with sweat-inducing mainland heat. Below is a list of the most celebrated dishes born from this cultural synthesis.

